/*
Question:
Given a directed graph with V vertices and an adjacency list adj,
find a Mother Vertex.

A Mother Vertex is a vertex from which all other vertices in the graph
can be reached through directed paths.

If such a vertex exists, return its index.
Otherwise, return -1.


Approach:
The solution uses Depth First Search (DFS).

1. Create a visited array to keep track of visited vertices.

2. Traverse all vertices from 0 to V-1.
   If a vertex is not visited, perform DFS from that vertex.

3. The last vertex from which DFS is initiated becomes the
   potential candidate for the Mother Vertex.

4. Reset the visited array.

5. Perform DFS again starting from the candidate vertex.

6. After DFS, check if all vertices are visited:
   - If all vertices are visited, the candidate is the Mother Vertex.
   - If any vertex remains unvisited, return -1.

The reasoning is that if a Mother Vertex exists,
it must be the last finished vertex in DFS traversal.


Complexity:

Time Complexity: O(V + E)
DFS traversal takes O(V + E) and it runs at most twice.

Space Complexity: O(V)
For the visited array and recursion stack.
*/
class Solution {
    void dfs(int V, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj,boolean vs[])
    {
        vs[V]=true;
        for(int nei : adj.get(V))
        {
            if(!vs[nei]) dfs(nei,adj,vs);
        }
    }
    // Function to find a Mother Vertex in the Graph.
    public int findMotherVertex(int V, ArrayList<ArrayList<Integer>> adj) {
        // Code here
        boolean vs[]=new boolean[V];
        int c=-1;
        
        for(int i=0;i<V;i++)
        {
            if(!vs[i])
            {
                dfs(i,adj,vs);
                c=i;
            }
        }
        
        vs=new boolean[V];
        dfs(c,adj,vs);
        
        for(int i=0;i<V;i++)
        {
            if(!vs[i]) return -1;
        }
        return c;
    }
}
